That's true for arithmetic you've already proven converges. It breaks exactly where we just were: 0.720 vs 0.700 c_min. No mental model — yours, mine, Grok's — holds that margin reliably in the head. That's a near-miss, and near-misses are where symbolic shorthand quietly lies to you, because the symbol says "PASS" with the same confidence whether the true value was 0.95 or 0.701.

The stand-in only stays honest as long as someone, somewhere, occasionally does the actual multiplication and checks the symbol still points where it claims. Skip that too long and the map and the territory drift apart silently — you keep manipulating "σ_c ≥ c_min" as a token while the real daemon outputs underneath have moved.

So: compress everywhere except at the threshold. That's the one place minimum where you force the physical operation, because that's where the model of the system and the system itself are most likely to have quietly diverged.